A||||8||Fret Naught||Advice Please||||5||
Z||000000||Fret Naught||05-05-2000||09:54 PM||||Here goes, I picked up my first Fiero about a month ago, I have conducted a compression test on the engine. To see what kind of shape it is in. 86 SE V6. The car has been gone through body wise, but no info on the engine.  Anyway, cylinders 1,3,5,2,4 all read appx. 150 + or - 5 pounds. I was getting excited thinking it must be somewhat fresh the Odometer reads 147K and I didn't think a 14 year old car with at least 147K would read that high or that consistant. Any way on to #6 it came in at 120 pounds far below criteria, add a little oil and boom up to 150 so I know it is not valves must be piston,ring, or cylinder. Here is the thing. not using any oil that I can tell, and the engine runs sooo smooth. I am inclined to just run it until problems arise. What are your thoughts? Opinions etc...||||reg||5
Z||000001||N_Man||05-05-2000||10:00 PM||||If it aint broke dont fix it.<BR>Unless, of course you want to <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||05-05-2000||10:54 PM||||my car has had 16 bottles of nitrous run through it with 97,000 on the odo and shes still fine... these 60* V6s ROCK!  If i may borrow a phrase from timex.. "take a licken and keep on ticken.."  the one thing i did change internaly was the valve stem seals.. the puffy smoke on start up was rather tacky.. $14 later and 5 hours  it was over with..  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||bad88||05-05-2000||11:43 PM||||Have yoy tried a little slick 50 in there?  There was a company who made just the PTFE teflon coating (the wonder of Slick 50) for about a third the price.  I know after putting sonme in my Fiero the RPM was a little smoother and the idle wasn't quite as high nor was the RPM to get the same speed.  They claim that you can add it and it will improve performance and I'm pretty sure it will fill the gaps missed by the rings.  Not the "right" answer but it should keep you going a little longer till you can change those rings. Check your compression after about a week and see if it improves...at least it can't hurt.  If I remember the name of that straight PTFE I'll post it.  I'm pretty sure that most of the auto parts stores carry it though.  Hope that helps.||||reg||3
Z||000005||Linx||05-06-2000||01:53 AM||||Fret-ster,<BR>My haynes says that the minimum compression is 100 lbs.  Section 34 of chapter 1.  The lowest cylinder shouldnt be less than 70% of the highest.  Man- your engine's just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||05-06-2000||07:03 AM||||There was a thread on the site about not using the PTFE material in your engine.  The PTFE is a suspended particulate component of these lubricants.  It does fill the spaces, and the spaces you don't want filled as this material settles out.  The report did not recommend any of the additives and warned of the hazards.  Snake Oil for the 21st century.||||reg||13
Z||000007||Fret Naught||05-06-2000||12:11 PM||||Thanks Linx,<BR>I read that to, the thing that worries me is the part that says there should be no greater difference of 12 - 15 pounds between any two cylinders.<P>Lar I already put in some restore I hope that isn't the same stuff you are talking about. Since then I have noticed real high oil pressure (intermitently) pegs the scale then eventually drops off ||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||05-06-2000||12:44 PM||||Sounds to me like you have a very solid engine that my have been rebuilt, or at least had the oil pump replaced. I know my engine was running pretty strong at 130k miles but oil pressure would never really stay up there. So when I threw a rod through the side of my block I guess I shouldn't have been suprised.  But think about it.. 130k miles on a mid-eighties domestic engine with little work other than regular oil changes and tune-ups. And "they" say Fieros are crap. Go figure.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||05-06-2000||10:14 PM||||My oil pressure runs around 35 at idle when warmed up, and 40 when driving.  165,000 miles, and no more than an oil change every 2-3000 miles, 10W-40 Havoline 3 in summer, and 5W-30 in winter when I lived up north.  I've used Slick 50, once at 90,000, and agin at 120.  I also used a Fram Extraguard filter with the PTFE in it once around 15,000 miles ago, so I didn't have to mix and match the oils.  I don't see them around any more, though.  Noticed the smoother idle initially, but no real difference after a short time.<BR>As far as the oil pressure going high, my original sending unit caused this occasionally, and the Pontiac dealer told me this was common for the sending units to stick.||||reg||1
